---
/**
 * /about — the person behind burningsuit.
 *
 * Copy is the SIGNED deck at design/copy/about.md (v5, signed 2026-06-17, with
 * Alan's inline edits; +the LinkedIn line, Alan's own words, signed 2026-08-19).
 * Voice: warm outward, light about himself, "for everyone else" as the spine,
 * spoken cadence. Structure: prose chapters on the dark ground — page-hero →
 * thread → chapters → thread-end. The visible LinkedIn link mirrors
 * FOUNDER.sameAs in site.ts (schema and page agree on the same URL).
 *
 * PHOTOS (2026-07 review: three text chapters passed before the first image):
 * the "we're all middle management now" talk shot sits in "the part that isn't
 * really technical" — beside the very sentence that names the talk (the café
 * shot stays homepage-only; Alan: no doubling). Stuart & Alison's studio shot
 * sits in "where burningsuit comes from" (clearances in the ops claims
 * ledger), and Zelda closes "and the dog". Sequence: talk → origin → dog.
 */
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Signoff from "../components/Signoff.astro";
import { Picture } from "astro:assets";
import zelda from "../assets/photos/zelda.jpg";
import mmnTalk from "../assets/photos/mmn-talk.jpg";
import parents from "../assets/photos/stuart-alison.jpg";

const contactSubject = "Hello";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About - burningsuit: Power BI, Fabric & AI for everyone else"
  description="I'm Alan Harman-Box. I work alongside teams on Power BI, Fabric and AI, drawing on a decade in software delivery. Here's how I work and where burningsuit comes from."
  contactSubject={contactSubject}
>
  <Fragment slot="footer-invitation"
    >If any of this sounds like the kind of thing you're dealing with, I'd
    genuinely like to hear about it</Fragment
  >

  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1 class="enter appear-300">Power BI and AI - for everyone <em>else</em>.</h1>
      <p class="lead enter appear-450">
        I'm Alan Harman-Box, and burningsuit is me. I work alongside teams on Power BI and Fabric, helping them build the reporting they need or take ownership of what someone else built. I'm interested in the people who just want to get their job done well. I'm more than happy to be the nerd in the room, so you don't have to.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="thread" data-reveal-raw aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="capable">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="section-head" id="capable" data-reveal>I start from what people can do</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p data-reveal>Before working with organisations on Power BI and Fabric, I spent about a decade in software delivery - large systems, some of it for government. The nuts and bolts matter, but helping the team understand its own reporting is what I use that experience for.</p>
        <p data-reveal>
          I've got a fairly stubborn belief that, with the right instruction and
          a bit of encouragement, almost anyone can learn almost anything - so I
          won't turn up assuming a team's behind, or that the people are the
          problem. Most of the teams I meet are more capable than they've been
          given credit for; they're just busy with the day job, a bit wary of
          the latest thing, and short on someone to think it through with.
        </p>
        <p data-reveal>
          In practice, I teach the next useful skill, give the team time to apply it and help with the questions that come back. We can use representative examples or the live system, according to what the team needs. The <a class="u" href="/power-bi/">Power BI page explains how we can work together</a>. If you'd sooner see the results, there are <a class="u" href="/work/">a few case studies</a>. And I'm on
          <a class="u" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/alan-harman-box"
          target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LinkedIn</a>, if that's
          where you check people out.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="human">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="section-head" id="human" data-reveal>The part that isn't really technical</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p data-reveal>
          What makes the difference is whether people who are busy, and a bit sceptical,
          actually feel like having a go - and whether, by the end, your team
          reckons I get how they work, rather than someone turning up to tell
          them they've been doing it wrong. That's the part I care about
          getting right.
        </p>
        <p data-reveal>
          In 2026, I gave a talk at the Manchester Power BI User Group called "we're all middle management now", about being responsible for work you've handed off to something that can't be held responsible for it. I've also given it at Bristol and DataSwindon. The thing that's stuck
          with me through all of it is that these projects mostly succeed or
          fail on the people side, well before the technology really comes into
          it.
        </p>
        {/* The talk the paragraph above names, as it actually happens —
            evidence beside its claim. Clearance row in the ops ledger. */}
        <figure class="figure-inset" data-reveal-fig>
          <div class="ph">
            <Picture
              src={mmnTalk}
              alt="Alan giving the 'we're all middle management now' talk at a user group, the title slide up beside him"
              formats={["avif"]}
              fallbackFormat="webp"
              widths={[700, 1050, 1400]}
              sizes="(min-width: 56rem) 42rem, 100vw"
              quality={60}
              loading="lazy"
            />
          </div>
          <figcaption class="ph-note">The talk, live - we're all middle management now</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="origin">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="section-head" id="origin" data-reveal>Where burningsuit comes from</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p data-reveal>
          burningsuit was my parents' Power BI training business. They handed it
          over and they're properly retired now, so it's me. I've
          taken it somewhere a bit different from where it started - these days
          it's more about advising and designing alongside teams than running
          courses - but the reason it exists hasn't really changed.
        </p>
        <p data-reveal>
          As my mum said, burningsuit was for "everyone else" - normal people,
          basically. Not the nerds, not the people who are always online
          performing their work - just someone trying to get a job done. And
          that's still the focus.
        </p>
        <figure class="figure-inset" data-reveal-fig>
          <div class="ph">
            <Picture
              src={parents}
              alt="Stuart and Alison, who ran burningsuit before Alan, at a laptop together - Alison pointing at the screen"
              formats={["avif"]}
              fallbackFormat="webp"
              widths={[700, 1050, 1400]}
              sizes="(min-width: 56rem) 42rem, 100vw"
              quality={60}
              loading="lazy"
            />
          </div>
          <figcaption class="ph-note">Stuart &amp; Alison - the original Power BI people</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="dog">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="section-head" id="dog" data-reveal>And the dog</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p data-reveal>
          Last thing: this is Zelda, who I'm told is Chief Morale Officer. She's
          no use at all with the DAX, but she's very good at making me get up and
          go outside now and then, which honestly does more for the work than
          you'd think.
        </p>
        <figure class="figure-inset" data-reveal-fig>
          <div class="ph">
            <Picture
              src={zelda}
              alt="Zelda, a small black-and-tan dog, snuggled into a cream fleece blanket"
              formats={["avif"]}
              fallbackFormat="webp"
              widths={[700, 1050, 1400]}
              sizes="(min-width: 56rem) 42rem, 100vw"
              quality={60}
              loading="lazy"
            />
          </div>
          <figcaption class="ph-note">Chief Morale Officer, on duty</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <Signoff reveal />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="thread thread-end" data-reveal-raw aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <div class="talk-spacer"></div>
</BaseLayout>
